567483bb9c72278","e567483bb9c72278","e6dc29dac929fa79","e782ebe4cd31b23b","e78a7b202f9ad689","e9fb83decf89c718","ea2ddc2fdb2af423","ec092049266191d6","ecf0a847234ff999","ed62eb2ec785e04f","eda8404e47766c9a","eec3907d67aeccae","eefd7e16a9aec4ad","ef4a5ed7ca473c2f","ef4a5ed7ca473c2f","f0df147d06f567fe","f10f08d8cb083119","f14c68e53eccc825","f1bdc7afd5e8e00f","f33b0f13e3072b08","f413a47e2b992b0f","f8471183d76cd8ec","f8afb1651c37dd48","fb3e80229c69c661","fb3e80229c69c661","fb3e80229c69c661","fd48c1798ee1923a","fdc6c94f8175d220","fe2b9d97f40c6f22","fe772bd3701dcce0"],"groups":["d90aba77d022eaaf","ac447138e55f8a47","4e880f77ceecf1ba","af042524db376fb5","cdc5603da00c61b2","c103075f60052dac","7d45851e347db427","93a17abf38a72b84","d6c370c50b9515a7","fb7c2b64b79999f4","f49d0251952d6325","ab0c498645018891","81a0ed48d50835ab","74919cca56462bf3","3e4d3236bbe70359","c9738f9fbe053a75"],"findings":"**Summary:** The suite change works. The full `npm test` passed (284/284, about 192 s). But `dumpDom` does not kill its process group on timeout, which contradicts the `chromium-lifetime` and `docs` annotations.\n\n### 1. Mismatch: `dumpDom` leaks its browser tree on timeout (`chromium-lifetime`, `docs`) — **verified**\n\n`test/browser.ts` imports `spawnSync` from `./run.ts`. On timeout, that wrapper throws `timed out after N ms` before `dumpDom` reaches `killGroup(r.pid)`. The group kill never runs.\n\nProbe: a stub browser (`GLOSS_CHROMIUM`) that does `trap \"\" TERM; sleep 100`, called with `dumpDom(url, { timeout: 1000 })`.\n- It threw `timed out after 1000 ms: timeout --signal=KILL 600 …` after 1012 ms.\n- `sleep 100` was still running afterwards. I killed it by hand; it had inherited the ignored TERM, so it needed SIGKILL.\n\nThe `timeout --signal=KILL 600` bound does not help here either. Node SIGKILLs the `timeout` wrapper itself, so nothing remains to enforce the 600 s bound.\n\nThe annotation says \"dumpDom kills the group afterwards\", and the `docs` TODO says \"dumpDom kills its group\". Both are false for the timeout path. The stub test in `hangguard.test.ts` only covers the non-timeout exit, so it cannot catch this.\n\nFix: kill the group whether or not the call timed out. For example, use the raw `node:child_process` `spawnSync` in `dumpDom`, or catch the wrapper's error and kill by the pid it still has. Then add a timeout case to the stub test.\n\n### 2. Necessity grading — `chromium-lifetime` (required) and `suite-timeouts` / `file-backstop`\n\n- **`chromium-lifetime` is fairly graded as required.** The reason for the coreutils `timeout` wrapper is that nothing in the test process runs after a SIGKILL. I checked the wrapper's basic behaviour: a backgrounded helper in the same group died at expiry — **verified**. I did not check that Chromium's own helpers stay in that group. That is **read** only, and it is untested in the suite.\n- **`file-backstop` (supporting) is accurate.** A scratch test hanging on an active interval, run with `--test-timeout=1000 --import test/guard.ts` and `GLOSS_FILE_TIMEOUT=4000`, was cancelled at the test timeout. The process then ended at 4 s with the backstop message and exit 1 — **verified**. The `--import` reaches test-file subprocesses.\n- **`suite-timeouts` (required)** is fine. `--test-timeout` is the only thing that fails a hung in-process test within 180 s rather than 10 min.\n\n### 3. Other checks\n\n- **`child-limits` / `split()` (required): accurate.** The `hangguard` default-timeout test passes, including the explicit `undefined` options argument — **verified**.\n- **`route-imports` (supporting): accurate.** No test file still imports `node:child_process` directly. The remaining raw imports are in `src/` and `scripts/` — **read**.\n- **`guard-test` (supporting): accurate.** All four `hangguard` tests pass in 40 s, and the browse-timeout and process-group cases are included — **verified**. The red-check against a parent-only kill was not re-run — **read** only.\n- **`src/git.ts` has no timeout** on its raw `execFileSync` (line 29) — **read**. This matches the author's risk note.\n\n### 4. Removal candidates / simplifications\n\nNone worth dropping. The `dumpDom` fix in item 1 is the one change I would make.\n\n### 5. Missing risks\n\n- A `dumpDom` timeout is the case where the wrapper's bound is lost, as above.\n- `browse()` steps keep running after the deadline, as the author noted; I did not test this beyond the 30 s stub-test linger.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES\n\nThat notification is for the earlier background wait on the full `npm test` run. It finished with exit 0, and it matches the result I already reported: 284 pass, 0 fail. Nothing has changed since my review, so the report above still stands. The one issue to fix is still the `dumpDom` timeout path, which leaks the browser process group.","verdict":"issues"}
