st.ts` passes both tests in about 7 s, so the relative `--import` path and the guard load under the package flags. Chromium is at `/usr/bin/chromium`, so the browser test ran rather than skipped. I did not run the full suite, the SIGKILL bound, or the \"--test-timeout alone does not end the file\" claim. The rest is **read** from the code.\n\n## 1. Mismatches\n\n- **`chromium-lifetime` and `docs` (read):** The TODO says \"Chromium runs under `timeout --signal=KILL 600` … (bounds it after a SIGKILLed test run)\". That is only true for `browse()`. `dumpDom` (test/browser.ts:43-50) calls `spawnSync(chromium!, …)` directly, not through `lifetime()`, and is `detached: true`. If the test process is SIGKILLed during a dump, that Chromium group has no bound. Low risk, because `--dump-dom` normally exits on its own, but the claim is broader than the code.\n- **`child-limits` (read, latent bug):** `split()` in test/run.ts treats a trailing `undefined` as a non-options argument. For `spawnSync(cmd, args, undefined)`, `rest` becomes `[cmd, args, undefined]` and the call becomes `spawnSync(cmd, args, undefined, opts)`. Node ignores the fourth argument, so the 120 s timeout and SIGKILL are silently dropped. No current call site I found does this. The fix is to strip a trailing `undefined` before the options check.\n- **`child-limits` notes (read):** \"every test child gets a 120 s timeout\" also covers the async `spawn` calls for long-lived `ary` servers (test/ary.test.ts:41, 184, 194, 210; test/untrusted.test.ts:286). Any such test that runs past 120 s gets its server SIGKILLed mid-test. The author's notes don't say this.\n\n## 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- **`route-imports` [mechanical] should be [supporting] serving `child-limits`.** The import swap is the only thing that makes the guard apply to any test. It has design content, and the \"if removed\" text says as much.\n- **`browse()` deadline (in `chromium-lifetime`) is partly defensive.** The task (\"fail after a bounded time\") needs a bound, and the 180 s `--test-timeout` already provides one. The 100 s deadline adds a specific message and an earlier Chromium kill. Calling it required is fair, but its unique value is smaller than the grouping suggests.\n- **`file-backstop` [supporting]** is correctly graded. Its \"verified\" claim is one I did not re-run.\n\n## 3. Removal candidates\n\n- **`dumpDom` `killGroup` (read):** `--dump-dom` exits by itself, so killing the group after it returns is defensive. Removing it has low impact, but it is cheap and does cover helpers after a timeout, so keeping it is also reasonable.\n- **`hasTimeout` / `lifetime()` wrapper (read):** This is the only bound on Chromium after a SIGKILLed test. It is not a removal candidate for the task as stated. It is the most fragile part (platform detection, and the bound is untested per the author's own risk list).\n\n## 4. Simplifications\n\n- **`split()` (test/run.ts):** Strip a trailing `undefined` first (`if (a.at(-1) === undefined) a = a.slice(0, -1)`), then use the existing check. This fixes the latent bug above.\n- **`browse()` `run()` lifetime:** `run()` is left running after the deadline rejects. A flag checked in `waitFor`, or a `signal` passed to the steps, would stop it. Right now it keeps polling a dead port for up to `waitFor`'s 30 s.\n\n## 5. Missing risks\n\n- **Grandchild processes (read):** `run.ts` SIGKILLs only the direct child. `src/git.ts` runs git via raw `execFileSync`, so a hung `git` under a `git-gloss` child survives the kill. If it holds the stdout/stderr pipe, `spawnSync` can still block past its timeout. This is the same class of hang the change claims to fix, and it is not in the risk list.\n- **`browse()` steps keep running after the deadline (read):** See the simplification above. A step can still act on the page or the temp profile after `browse()` has rejected and the profile is being cleaned up.\n- **Raw `node:child_process` in product code (read):** `src/git.ts`, `src/render.ts`, `src/commands/init.ts`, `src/commands/html.ts` and `scripts/build.mjs` are not covered by the helpers. The author notes this for new tests, but not for code the tests run through `BIN`.\n\nNothing was changed or committed, so no gloss step was needed.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
